dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, ms. thompson. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay.
[doctor] so i see that you're here for joint pain. have you had any injury or trauma to your knee?
[patient] no, i've just been swimming more and trying to be more active. my knee starts hurting after a couple of laps. i could swim ten laps before, but now it's been hurting.
[doctor] okay. and how about your back pain, is that still bothering you?
[patient] it's been intermittent, but right now it's been doing well.
[doctor] okay. and your depression, how are you doing with that?
[patient] it's been going really well with the medication and therapy. i'm trying to get outside more and it's been helping me with that. i'm sleeping and eating well. i'm not having any concerns about hurting myself or others.
[doctor] that's great. i'm glad to hear that. i'm gon na go ahead and examine you. so, i do see that you have a history of carpal tunnel release. how is that doing?
[patient] it's doing really well. i've been getting full range of motion and it feels a lot better.
[doctor] okay. so i'm gon na go ahead and examine you now. i'm gon na be calling out some of my findings as i go. so on your physical exam, i do appreciate your mood and affect. your grips are equal bilaterally. on your neck exam, it's supple without thyromegaly or cervical lymphadenopathy. your lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. your heart exam is regular rate and rhythm. no murmurs, gallops, or rubs. no extra heart sounds. i do appreciate some lower extremity edema. and on your knee exam, you do have some pain with patellar inhibition. and you do have some pain with range of motion of the right knee.
[patient] mm-hmm.
[doctor] so i did order an x-ray of your right knee. it's unremarkable. so what i'm thinking with your right knee pain, i think you have chondromalacia of the right patella. it's just some cartilage wear and tear. have you noticed any injury or trauma to your knee?
[patient] no, i've just been swimming more.
[doctor] okay. and you're doing well with swimming?
[patient] yeah, i love swimming.
[doctor] okay. so i'm gon na prescribe you some ibuprofen 600 milligrams four times a day with food. i want you to do some straight leg raising exercises that will strengthen your quadriceps. i would encourage you to participate in physical activity that's less straining on your joints, such as swimming freestyle laps or to stop activity altogether. if your pain does not improve in a couple of weeks, we will follow up. does that sound okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm gon na go ahead and put you down for a follow-up in two weeks. so for your back pain, it sounds like that's stable and well controlled at this time. so we're not going to change anything with that. and for your depression, i want you to continue your current medications. do you have any questions?
[patient] no, i think that's it.
[doctor] okay. and for your carpal tunnel, i'm just gon na keep you on your current regimen with no changes at this time. any other questions?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. well, it was nice to see you today. have a great day.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] thank you. }}<|eot_id|>
