dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi jacqueline good to see you today
[patient] good to see you too
[doctor] so i know that you have a history of perioral dermatitis and rosacea including ocular rosacea so how are you doing with your perioral dermatitis
[patient] it's well controlled but it's not gone away completely
[doctor] okay so you were using the doxycycline twice a day
[patient] i was but my pharmacist said i could cut back to once a day since it was improving so i've been doing that for about a week now
[doctor] okay and has it continued to improve or has it worsened at all
[patient] it's not gotten any better but it's not gotten any worse
[doctor] okay
[patient] and i'm still using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day and it's really helped my skin is much improved moisturization
[doctor] okay and you're using the metrocream after the sulfacetamide wash
[patient] yes i am
[doctor] okay and have you tried any other lotions or creams or anything like that
[patient] no i'ven't i've been trying to stay away from that
[doctor] okay and what about the cetaphil lotion or soap or the neutrogena wipes
[patient] no i haven't tried any of those
[doctor] okay and i know you were thinking about using elidel
[patient] yeah i was but it's not covered by my insurance and i was worried about some of the side effects
[doctor] okay
[patient] so i decided to pass on that
[doctor] okay alright so let's take a look at your skin now i know you've been using the sulfacetamide wash and the metrocream so let's take a look at your skin now
[doctor] okay so i'm just gon na do a quick skin inspection of your face and i do see on your bilateral medial cheeks a few very faint erythematous papules with some mild redness around and underneath the nostrils and your fitzpatrick skin type is a four to five
[patient] okay
[doctor] so let's talk a little bit about your perioral dermatitis so it's improved but it has not resolved so i'm gon na recommend that you go back to using the doxycycline twice a day and i know you've been using it once a day and it has improved but it's not fully resolved so let's go back to twice a day and then once it's fully resolved you can reduce it to once a day for about a week and then you can kind of maintain that once a day for a little while longer and then you can try to stop it altogether but we'll follow up in four weeks and see how you're doing
[patient] okay
[doctor] and i know you were not going to use elidel for financial reasons so i do have another option for you it's called tacrolimus and it's a topical steroid that's very helpful for rosacea and perioral dermatitis so i can prescribe that for you if you're interested
[patient] i would like to try that
[doctor] okay so i'll write you a prescription for that and it's very important that you use it exactly as i'm telling you to use it and don't overuse it because it can have some side effects like it can lighten your skin and make it thinner so it's very important that you only use it exactly as directed
[patient] okay
[doctor] and i know you're still using the sulfacetamide wash once a day
[patient] yes
[doctor] and you're using the metrocream twice a day
[patient] yes
[doctor] okay so you can continue with that and then for your ocular rosacea i do want you to gently wash your eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser and then you can use a gentle cream like vanicream and i know you were using the bruder mask as needed so you can continue with that as well
[patient] okay
[doctor] alright any questions for me
[patient] no
[doctor] okay great if you have any issues or if your condition worsens please do give us a call and we'll see you back in four weeks
[patient] thank you
[doctor] you're welcome }}<|eot_id|>
