dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, mr. smith. how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay. i'm here for my abdominal pain.
[doctor] okay. so, mr. smith, i know that you've had some abdominal pain, and i'm gon na go ahead and examine you. but i have to tell you, the notes indicate that you've had some history of type 2 diabetes. is that correct?
[patient] yes, that's correct.
[doctor] okay. and have you been managing that with diet and exercise?
[patient] yeah, i'm trying to.
[doctor] okay. and i know that you enjoy photography, and i'm sure that you've been out taking some nice pictures of nature. what kind of photography do you like to do?
[patient] i like to do landscape photography.
[doctor] okay. that's really nice. i love photography myself. i love taking pictures of my kids, but i'm not very good at it. okay. so, let's talk a little bit about your symptoms. you've had this abdominal pain. tell me where it hurts and when it hurts.
[patient] it hurts on the right side and it hurts when i eat.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any nausea or vomiting?
[patient] yeah, i've had a little bit of nausea.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any low-grade fevers?
[patient] no, not really.
[doctor] okay. and have you had this pain for a long time or is it recent?
[patient] it's been going on for about a week or so.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any family history of gallstones or cholecystitis?
[patient] yeah, both of my parents have had cholecystectomies.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm just gon na go ahead and check your vitals. your blood pressure is 128 over 88, your heart rate is 68, your respiratory rate is 16, and your oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. and your temperature is afebrile. so, let's go ahead and examine you. okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] okay. so, i'm just gon na examine your abdomen. it's soft and nontender, but i do appreciate some slight guarding to the right upper quadrant, but without rebound tenderness. and i do appreciate murphy's sign, which is consistent with cholecystitis. i do appreciate bowel sounds in all four quadrants. and on heart exam, your heart is regular rate and rhythm, no ectopic beats, no murmurs, clicks, or rubs. on lung exam, your lungs are clear and equal bilaterally. so, we did a review of the ultrasound that you had at the outside facility, and it showed that you do have multiple gallstones in your gallbladder, and there is some mild thickening of the wall of the gallbladder. but the common bile duct is within normal limits at 4.1 millimeters. so, based on your symptoms, exam findings, and the ultrasound, i do believe that you have some mild cholecystitis with gallstones. i don't appreciate any frank obstruction, but it looks like your gallbladder is inflamed. this appears to have happened maybe over the last few weeks or since you've been symptomatic. so, for that, i think that the best course of action would be to do a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. i think that this will resolve your symptoms. i'm gon na go ahead and schedule that surgery for next week.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] i don't think that you'll have any complications, and i think that you'll do very well. and i'll see you in a week after the surgery. and i want you to refrain from hiking in the immediate postoperative period. but you can go back to walking outdoors on solid ground a week after the surgery. and i think that you should follow up with your physician regarding your impending surgery. and we'll continue to monitor your diabetes as has been outlined by either your endocrinologist or your primary care provider.
[patient] okay. that sounds good.
[doctor] okay. any questions?
[patient] no, that's it.
[doctor] okay. great. and i'll see you next week. thank you.
[patient] thank you.
[doctor] hey, dragon, show me the video. }}<|eot_id|>
