dialogue is: {{ [doctor] hi, jeremy, how are you?
[patient] i'm doing okay, how about you?
[doctor] i'm doing okay. so i see that you're here for low back pain. tell me, when did this start?
[patient] it started about four months ago, and i was moving boxes out of my son's college dorm, and i felt this tingling in my right toes, but that went away in about an hour. but after that, i felt like i couldn't support my upper body weight, and i've been having weakness in my legs when i'm carrying heavy objects.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any pain since then?
[patient] yeah, i have a sharp pain in my lower back when i turn a certain way or roll over in my sleep, but it goes away with repositioning.
[doctor] okay. and have you tried anything for the pain?
[patient] yeah, i've been taking tylenol and ibuprofen, and i've been using heat and ice, but they don't really help.
[doctor] okay. and which one helps more?
[patient] the ibuprofen is a little more beneficial than the tylenol, but it still doesn't relieve the symptoms.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any upset stomach with the ibuprofen?
[patient] no, i haven't had any upset stomach with it.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any physical therapy?
[patient] i tried some physical therapy at the gym, but it didn't help.
[doctor] okay. and any bladder or bowel issues?
[patient] no, no issues with that.
[doctor] okay. and have you had any fevers or chills?
[patient] no, i haven't had any fevers or chills.
[doctor] okay. and i see here in your history that you had a knee surgery two years ago, how's that doing?
[patient] it feels 95 percent better.
[doctor] that's great to hear. and have you had any other surgeries or procedures?
[patient] no, just the knee surgery.
[doctor] okay. and i'm just gon na do a quick physical exam, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so on your exam, i'm feeling around your neck, and i'm feeling a bony protuberance of c-5 through c-6, but it's not painful to palpation of the c-5 through c-6, and your reflexes are normal.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] on your lower back exam, there's no pain to palpation of the lumbar spine, but you have decreased flexion and extension of the lower back, and you have a positive straight leg raise, and you have spasm of the paraspinal muscles of the lumbar spine, and your strength is equal bilaterally.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i reviewed your x-rays, and they did not show any acute bony abnormalities.
[patient] okay.
[doctor] so based on my exam and your radiographic findings, i think you may have a possible acute disc herniation, and i'm gon na order an mri of your lumbar spine, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] and i'm gon na prescribe you meloxicam 15 milligrams a day to treat your pain, and i'm gon na give you a referral for physical therapy, and you can continue with that, okay?
[patient] okay.
[doctor] any questions?
[patient] no, i don't think so.
[doctor] okay. jeremy, the medical assistant will be in soon to get you checked out, okay?
[patient] okay. }}<|eot_id|>
